  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nSouthwesterly flow is presently overhead, bringing with it\nwell-above average temperatures and very little cloud cover,\nmaking for a downright pleasant mid-November afternoon. These\ncalm and comfortable conditions will be short-lived, however, as\nour next upper-level trough and associated cold front will \nbegin to approach our area during the day Friday. Our first\nnoticeable impact from this system will be a return of gusty\nwinds beginning around midday and lasting through the evening.\nSouthwesterly 850-mb flow will increase to 50-60 kt by early\nafternoon, resulting in surface wind gusts approaching or\nperhaps locally exceeding 40-45 MPH. Should stratus linger\nlonger than anticipated, winds may end up a little slower than\nforecast. In coordination with neighboring WFOs, we have \nelected not to issue a wind advisory at this time, but later \nshifts will continue to monitor. \n\nThe surface cold front will enter our northwestern counties\naround 06Z Saturday and progress through our area during\nthroughout the following 24 hours. In its wake, surface\ntemperatures are expected to drop into the low-mid 40s. For\nthose in southeastern NE/western IA, this will mean a 10-15\ndegree drop from morning high temperatures to evening lows.\n\nThe upper-level trough will deepen as it moves east of the\nRockies Saturday afternoon, aiding in rainfall development \nalong and just behind the passing cold front (60-80%). This \narea of rain is not expected to be particularly heavy, but with \nCAPE values on the order of a few hundred J/kg in our far \nsoutheastern counties, a few rumbles of thunder along with \nlocally enhanced rainfall rates are possible there.\n\nBehind the front, a seasonably chilly airmass will take hold\nbeginning on Sunday, with high temperatures running 5-10 \ndegrees below climatological norms. While flow aloft will \nremain largely zonal for the rest of the period, a weak trough\nmay pass near or just south/west of our area on Tuesday, though\nlittle to no precipitation is expected.",
